== A Husband’s Sincere and Steady Love for His Wife

=== §. 61. #emph[Of a husband’s first beginning to love his wife.]

Now that the general #emph[matter,] together with the particular #emph[kinds,] of husbands’ duties has been handled thus far, the #emph[manner] of performing them must also be set out.

To instruct a husband in the #emph[manner] of performing his duties to his wife, the apostle lays down two patterns:

+ #emph[Christ,] v. 25.
+ #emph[Ourselves,] v. 28.

As Christ loves his church, and as we love ourselves, so men must love their wives.

So that we may follow these patterns the better, we must note point by point how Christ loves his church, and how we #footnote[The manner of a husband’s love set forth by Christ’s manner of loving his church, in six branches. 1. Christ #emph[first] loves the church.] love ourselves.

The love of Christ for his church is commended to us in six distinct points, which are its #emph[order], its #emph[truth], its #emph[cause], its #emph[quality], its #emph[quantity], and its #emph[continuance].

I. As for the #emph[order,] Christ began to love his church: he showed his love to her before she loved him. As the air heated by the sun is hot, and a wall that the sunbeams strike gives a reflection of heat back again, so the church, heated as it were and warmed at heart by the sense of Christ’s love, loved him. The apostle expressly notes this (“We love Him #footnote[1 John 4:19.] because He first loved us”), and the church herself acknowledges it, saying, “Because of the fragrance of your perfume” #footnote[Song 1:2.] (by which we are revived and cheered) “the maidens love you.”

There is in us by nature no spark of love at all.#footnote[Husbands must by their love provoke their wives to love. Prov 2:17. אלוף] If Christ, by loving us first, did not instill love into us, we could no more love him than a living bird could rise out of a cold egg, if it were not kept warm by its mother sitting on it.

So a husband must be the first to begin to love his wife. His place of eminence and authority requires that he should be to his wife #emph[a guide,] a title expressly given to him by the Holy #footnote[#emph[Si caput est vir, praecedere debet in omnibus bonis factis vxorem suam, vt illa imitetur virum, & sequatur caput suum. Aug. de decem chor. c.] 3. \[If the husband is the head, he ought to go before his wife in all good deeds, so that she may imitate her husband and follow her head.\]] Spirit, to teach him to go before her, and by his example to instruct her and urge her on to do her duty. What a shame it would be for a man who is “the image and glory of God” and the “head” of his wife, in the same place toward her that Christ is toward his church, to be provoked to love her by his wife’s wifely conduct (she being the weaker vessel, under him, to learn from him)! #footnote[Treatise 1, §. 10.] There are reasons to stir up a wife to endeavor to go before her husband in doing her duty, and if she does, it is the greater glory to her; but this pattern of Christ should stir him much more to strive to go before her.

=== §. 62. #emph[Of husbands repaying unkindness for love.]

Contrary to this is the disposition of those who, having loving and dutiful wives, are nevertheless not at all moved to love them in return, but are as unkind and churlish as if they had the most irritable and perverse wives that could be. But what shall we say of those who love their wives the less, indeed hate them, for their eagerness to love and (as a testimony of true love) to perform every good duty? What can we say, but that they are nothing less than devils incarnate? For it is the devil’s way to overcome good with evil. These men make the doctrine of a wife’s subjection seem harsh, and the careful performance of it a heavy burden. They will never share in Christ’s love who in their place show themselves so unlike Christ.

=== §. 63. #emph[Of the] truth #emph[of husbands’ love.]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 57. 2. Christ loves in #emph[truth.]]

#smallcaps[II]. The #emph[truth] of Christ’s love was shown to his church by its fruits: “He gave Himself up for her.” It was therefore not in #emph[word] only, no, nor only in #emph[heart,] but in #emph[deed] also. So his love proved profitable and beneficial to his church, which by it was cleansed and made “a glorious #footnote[#emph[v.] 26, 27.] church.” Had he only borne a tender compassion and a pitying affection toward it, or labored only with comforting and sweet words to uphold and help it, it would still have lain polluted with sin, in the power of the devil and under God’s wrath, and so would have received no profit and benefit at all.

So husbands must love their wives in #emph[truth] and in #emph[deed.] #footnote[Husbands must love in #emph[deed.]] #footnote[1 John 3:18.] Such a love is required of a man toward his brother; much more, therefore, toward his wife, who is not only a #emph[sister] (as #footnote[1 Cor 9:5.] the apostle expressly calls her) but nearer than sister, mother, daughter, friend, or anyone else whatever. This therefore serves to press the #emph[practice] of all the duties named before that belong to a husband.

=== §. 64. #emph[Of husbands’ pretense.]

Contrary to this is the pretense and hypocrisy of those who make a great show of much love, and a pretense of earnest affection, using many outward compliments, but fail when they come to the truest test, the #emph[deed.] Some, like suitors or wooers, will promise #emph[mountains] but not perform #emph[molehills.] Others will hug and kiss their wives much, but trust them with nothing, nor provide them with the things they need. There are some who will weep much when their wives are sick, yet will not pay for medicine and such things for their recovery. Indeed, many will show a fair face toward their wives all their life long, and at their death leave them nothing to live on.

Hence it is that many who are reckoned by others to be very kind husbands are found by their wives to be far otherwise. If husbands’ love is tested by their practice and performance of the duties named before, it will be found that for the most part they come as far short in #emph[love] as wives do in #emph[subjection.]

=== §. 65. #emph[Of the] freeness #emph[of husbands’ love.]

#smallcaps[III]. The cause of Christ’s love was his love, as Moses notes: #footnote[3\. Christ loves #emph[freely.] Deut 7:7, 8.] “The #smallcaps[Lord] set His love on you#super[+] … because the #smallcaps[Lord] loved you#super[+].” His love arose only and wholly from himself, and was in every way free. As there was nothing in the church, before Christ loved her, to move him to love her, so there can be nothing that he could hope for afterward but what he himself bestowed. Indeed, he delights in the righteousness with which she is clothed as with a glorious robe, and in the heavenly graces with which she is adorned as with precious jewels. But that righteousness and those graces are his own, and of his free gift: “that He might present her to Himself a glorious church.” #footnote[Eph 5:27. Husbands’ love must be #emph[free.]]

In imitation of this, husbands should love their wives, though there were nothing in their wives to move them to do so but only that they are their wives; indeed, though no future benefit could afterward be expected from them. True love has regard to the #emph[object] that is loved, and the good it may do to it, rather than to the #emph[subject] that loves, and the good that it may receive. For love “does not seek its own.” #footnote[1 Cor 13:5.]

Christ’s love in this branch of it should further move husbands to do what lies in their power to make their wives worthy of love. So it will truly be said of them that they “dwell with their wives according to knowledge,”#footnote[1 Pet 3:7.] and so their love will appear to be, like Christ’s love, #emph[free.]

=== §. 66. #emph[Of husbands loving for advantage.]

Contrary to this is the love of those who love only for their own satisfaction and advantage. Many can love no further than they have some bait to allure their desires, such as beauty, wealth, honor, or the like ulterior motives; or at least the hope of some inheritance or portion beyond what they have, or of some favor that they expect from their wives’ families. This cannot be a true, sound love: such a man may be thought to love his wife’s beauty, inheritance, and family rather than his wife. This love cannot last.

=== §. 67. #emph[Of the] purity #emph[of husbands’ love.]

#smallcaps[IIII]. For its #emph[quality,] Christ’s love is a holy, pure, chaste #footnote[4\. Christ’s love #emph[pure.] Eph 5:26, 27.] love. As he himself is, so is his love, as is evident by its effect: for it moved him to “sanctify” and “cleanse” his church, to make it “a glorious church, not having spot.” He therefore in no way polluted or defiled his spouse. And so that his love might the better appear to be a chaste love, set only on one spouse and not on many, he united all his saints together by the #footnote[1 Cor 12:12, 13.] bond of his Spirit, and made them all “one body.”

By this husbands must learn to be so disposed toward #footnote[Husbands’ love must be #emph[chaste.] Heb 13:4.] their wives as is consistent with holiness and chastity. Though much love is required, yet it must not overflow those banks. “Marriage is honorable … and the bed undefiled.” It must therefore be used as an undefiled thing. This indeed belongs to the wife as well as to the husband. But because he is the head and guide of his wife, and ought to be a pattern and example before her, as Christ is before him, it is applied more especially to him. The purity of a husband’s love spoken of here has a double use:

1\. It restrains a husband’s love to his own wife. There is a general Christian love, by which every occasion of doing good is taken, with which a husband may and ought to love others; and there is a particular marriage love, by which he is moved to prefer his wife before all, and to have his heart set and fixed on her, so that it is hers and belongs only to her.

2\. It orders and moderates his love, so that it does not turn into sinful lust, by which that state (which in itself, by virtue of God’s ordinance, is holy) is polluted.

=== §. 68. #emph[Of husbands’ looseness.]

Contrary to this is not only #emph[adultery,] of which we have spoken #footnote[Treatise 2, part 2, §. 5, 8.] before, but also wantonness, looseness, and impurity with his wife. For many intemperate and unchaste husbands, giving the reins to their headstrong lusts, show as much unseemliness and plain filthiness toward their wives in their words, gestures, and actions (to say nothing of their thoughts, which are not seen) as others do toward prostitutes and harlots. This is a most shameless thing, and I am ashamed even to mention it; but because it is mentioned, let such men know that they will be counted among the “sexually immoral #footnote[Heb 13:4.] and adulterers” whom “God will judge.”

=== §. 69. #emph[Of husbands loving their wives more than themselves.]

The #emph[quantity] of Christ’s love cannot be expressed, for its #footnote[5\. Christ’s love extended to the giving of himself.] measure was beyond measure. “He gave Himself up”#footnote[Eph 5:25.] for his church, and in that respect he calls himself “the good shepherd” who “lays down His life for the sheep.”#footnote[John 10:11.] “Greater love has no one than this.”#footnote[John 15:13.] What will he who gave his life for her not do for his spouse?

This may seem too high a strain and pitch of love for a husband to attain to,#footnote[How husbands must love their wives more than themselves. 1 John 3:16.] a matter in which he must leave his pattern and not follow Christ. But Saint John extends the love of our brethren even this far: “We ought,” he says, “to lay down our lives for our brothers.” Therefore, by just consequence, for our wives. But so that this extent is not stretched too far, and husbands cast into a pit of needless peril, two cautions must be noted:

1\. That there must be an absolute necessity to bring us to this #footnote[Cautions about a husband’s loving his wife more than himself.] extremity of parting with our life: that is, when the good we aim at on behalf of our wives cannot be brought about any other way than by risking our life. There was no other way to redeem the church but by the blood of Christ.

2\. That the good we intend for our wives in this case must be of greater value than our temporal life, as the good of her soul is, the saving of it. So the apostle says, “I will most gladly spend and be spent for the sake of your#super[+] souls.”#footnote[2 Cor 12:15.] Men must carry this mind much more toward their wives. It was for our salvation that Christ gave himself.

=== §. 70. #emph[Of husbands’ unkindness.]

Contrary to this is the unkindness of those who prefer every trifle of their own before the good of their wives: their profit, their pleasure, their promotion draw their hearts and affections wholly away from their wives. If any extraordinary expense must be laid out, or pains taken, for their wives’ good, little love will then appear; by which it is plain that no true and sound love was settled in their hearts toward their wives. As gold and other such metals are tested by the fire, so love is tested by afflictions and crosses.

=== §. 71. #emph[Of combats under pretense of wives’ honor.]

Contrary to this, in the other extreme, is the overbold and headstrong pretended manhood of those husbands who, at every jealous suspicion and slight report, are ready to issue challenges to fight, and to enter into single combats and duels, under pretense of defending their wives’ honor. Since this is no justifiable way of righting a wrong, no honor can come to the wife by it, but much dishonor and danger to the husband. If he prevails over his adversary and kills him, he is made guilty of murder by it, and so reproach and shame must come on himself, his wife, and his whole family. If he is overcome and slain, his wife may be thought more guilty than she was before. And it often happens that God in just judgment gives the challenger over into his adversary’s hand, because he has undertaken so crooked a course.

=== §. 72. #emph[Of husbands’]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 61.] constancy #emph[in love.]

#smallcaps[VI].#footnote[6\. Christ’s love #emph[perpetual.]] The continuance of Christ’s love was without end: “Having loved His own … He loved them to the very end.”#footnote[John 13:1.] His love was constant (not by fits, now loving, then hating) and #emph[everlasting]#footnote[Hos 2:19.] (never repenting of it, never changing or altering his mind). No provocations, no transgressions could ever make him forget to love, and cease to do the good that he intended for his church. Note what he said to her even when she revolted from him: “You have played the harlot with many #footnote[Jer 3:1. 2 Sam 7:15.] lovers, yet return to Me”; and again, “My loving devotion will never be removed.”

For his love does not rest on the deserving of his church, but on the unchangeableness of his own will. As this showed Christ’s love to be a true, sound love, so it made it profitable and beneficial to the church, which, despite her many frailties, is glorified by virtue of it.

This last branch must be added to all the former branches #footnote[Husbands must be constant in their love.] of a husband’s love, or else they will all be in vain and to no purpose. This gives the truest test of sound love. Such was the love between David and Jonathan, the soundest love that ever was between one party and another. This brings the greatest glory to the party who loves, and the greatest benefit to the party who is loved. So that a man’s love may remain firm and inviolable in this way:

1\. He must be sure to lay a good foundation. He must ground his love on God’s ordinance, and love his wife because of the marriage bond that knits them together, and the close union that arises from it; and so it will last as long as that knot lasts.

2\. He must further support and strengthen it with an inviolable resolution to be changed and altered by no provocation, but rather to pass by all infirmities, endeavoring in love to remedy them if he possibly can; if not, to bear with them.

=== §. 73. #emph[Of husbands’ fickleness.]

Contrary to this is the fickleness of those whose love is ready to turn like a weathercock with every blast of a contrary wind: now tender-hearted, then hard-hearted again; now smiling, then scowling; now granting this and that favor, then denying everything, even such things as are needful.

Many whose love was as hot as fire while their wives were young, or their wives’ families were living, or while their wives pleased them, prove as cold as ice in their love when those occasions are taken away.

Again, others, after continuing for some time in doing good to their wives, think it a burden, and growing weary, give up their former good course altogether; which plainly shows that they never truly and entirely loved their wives.

By this pattern of Christ set before husbands here, we have on the one side a good direction to teach us how to love our wives, as has been declared in particular; and on the other side, matter for humbling, in that it shows us how far short we come of our bounden duty. Although wives may complain most of their burden, because it is a #emph[subjection,] to which by nature we are all loath to yield, yet I am sure #footnote[See Treatise 1, §. 10.] the heaviest burden is laid on the husband’s shoulders, and it is much easier to perform the part of a good wife than that of a good husband.

=== §. 74. #emph[Of husbands loving their wives as themselves.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 59.]

To the example of Christ the apostle adds the pattern of one’s self, in these words: “So men ought to love their #footnote[Eph 5:28.] wives as their own bodies.”

#emph[Question.] Is the former pattern not sufficient? Is this latter one more excellent, or more perfect?

#emph[Answer.] Christ’s example is a full, complete, perfect, and #footnote[To Christ’s example the pattern of one’s self is added.] in every way sufficient pattern, far more excellent than this of a man’s self. This one is not joined to it to add anything to that, or because of this pattern’s own excellence, but only because of our dullness, to make the point somewhat more plain and clear. #footnote[1\. Because it is more easily perceived.] For this pattern is more easily perceived and better discerned. Everyone knows how he loves his own body, but few or none know how Christ loves his church. Besides, #footnote[2\. Because it is more imitable.] that example of Christ may seem too high and excellent for anyone to attain to, even inimitable. Therefore, to show that he requires no more than a man may perform, if he will set himself with care and conscience to do his duty, he adds the pattern of #emph[one’s self:] what one does for his body, if he will, he may do for his wife.

No direction can be taken from this latter pattern that #footnote[The pattern of loving one’s self sets forth the same things that Christ’s love does.] might not be referred to the former, as most of the former (though in a far lowlier manner) may be referred to the latter. For the love that a man bears to himself is true and entire, without any pretense. The most dissembling wretch in the world (who in his dealings with other men does nothing uprightly) neither will nor can dissemble with himself. Though other men will never know the depth of his heart, yet “the spirit #footnote[1 Cor 2:11.] within him,” even himself, knows it. So this pattern also presses truth and sincerity on husbands in their affection toward their wives. Of all others, they must not dissemble and be double-dealing with their wives, but must let them know the wholeness of their affection toward them, and see that they neither fawn on them nor flatter them. Those who pretend great love to their wives in show only offend against nature itself. As this love of a man for his own self is, for its manner, entire and true, so it is also free, not forced; for its measure, as great as it can possibly be; and for its continuance, constant; and so it is like Christ’s love. But there are two points especially to be considered in the love of one’s self, which above others are most plainly discerned #footnote[Man is tender toward himself.] in this pattern: 1. #emph[Tenderness.] 2. #emph[Cheerfulness.]

No other man will or can handle a man’s hand, arm, leg, or any other part of his body as tenderly as he himself does: he feels his own pain keenly.

The metaphors that the apostle uses in these words, “nourishes and cherishes it,”#footnote[Eph 5:29.] vividly set forth this tenderness. For they are taken from fowls and birds, which very carefully and tenderly hover over their young, covering them all over with their wings and feathers, but so bearing up their own bodies that no weight lies on them.

So husbands ought, with all tenderness and mildness, #footnote[Husbands must deal tenderly with their wives.] to deal with their wives, as we have noted before in many particulars. Only, I thought it good to set this example of a man’s self before husbands as a vivid pattern, in which they might see an example without exception going before them, and by which they might receive excellent direction for the better performing of the particulars noted before.

Again, no friend, no parent, no other party will or can so #footnote[Man is cheerful in doing himself good.] willingly and cheerfully do any kindness for someone as a man does for himself. This, among others, is one special point that the law aims at when it commands a man, “Love your #footnote[Lev 19:18.] neighbor as yourself”: namely, as willingly and readily as he does for himself. Whatever a man does for himself, he does much more cheerfully than for another. No other proof is needed than experience. Let men take notice of their own mind and disposition when they do things for themselves, and this will be as clear as the light when the sun shines out at noonday.

Husbands ought to have such an affection toward their wives: #footnote[Husbands must be cheerful in seeking their wives’ good.] they ought to do anything for their wives more willingly and cheerfully than for parents, children, friends, or anyone else. Though this cheerfulness is an inward disposition of the heart, yet it may be shown by a man’s eagerness and readiness to do his wife good: when his wife no sooner desires a kindness than he is ready to grant it, as Boaz says to Ruth, “I will do for you whatever you request.”#footnote[Ruth 3:11.] Indeed, if by any means he may know that this or that will be needful for her, though she does not desire it, he will yet bring it about for her. This was the mind of the same Boaz toward Ruth, as the history shows in many particulars.

Contrary to this is the disposition of those husbands who do what they do on their wives’ behalf so grudgingly, so complainingly, and so discontentedly that their wives would rather it were not done at all. The manner of doing these things causes more grief to tender-hearted wives than the things themselves can do good.

So much for the manner that husbands ought to observe in performing their duties. The reasons to enforce it remain to be handled.

=== §. 75. #emph[Of Christ’s example, a motive to provoke husbands to love their wives.]

The examples named before, of #emph[Christ] and of #emph[ourselves,] as #footnote[General reasons. 1. Christ’s example.] they are patterns for our direction, so they are general motives to provoke and stir us up the more to perform all the duties named before in the manner prescribed.

No greater and stronger motive can be offered than the #footnote[Christ’s example the greatest motive that can be.] example of Christ. Example in itself has great force to provoke us to do anything, especially if it is the example of some great one, a man of rank and renown.

But who is greater than Christ? What pattern is more worthy? If (as was #footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 74.] shown) the example of the church has great force to move wives to be subject to their husbands, the example of Christ must have much greater force to move husbands to love their wives. It is a great honor to be like Christ, and his example is a perfect pattern.

There are two things in Christ’s example that are especially #footnote[Two things in Christ’s pattern provoke husbands to love their wives.] to be noted to move husbands to love their wives:

1\. The great inequality that is between him and his spouse.

2\. The small benefit that he reaps by loving her.

For the better discerning of that #emph[inequality,] the #emph[greatness] of #footnote[1\. The great inequality between Christ and his spouse.] Christ on the one side, and the #emph[lowliness] of the church on the other, must be duly weighed.

Christ’s #emph[greatness] is set forth in Scripture by comparing him with #emph[created things] and with the #emph[Creator.] Compared with created things, he is far more excellent than the most excellent, as the apostle proves by many arguments in the first chapter of Hebrews; that whole chapter is spent in proof of this point. And in another place it is said that he is set “far above all rule and authority, power and dominion, and every name that is named, not only in the present age but also in the one to come.”#footnote[Eph 1:21.]

Compared with the Creator, he is in no way inferior to him, but #emph[equal:]#footnote[Phil 2:6.] “the radiance of God’s glory and the exact representation of His nature,”#footnote[Heb 1:3.] and that #emph[Word]#footnote[John 1:1, 3.] of whom it is said, “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. … Through Him all things were made,” etc. So he is the very #emph[Creator] himself, #emph[eternal, infinite, incomprehensible.] So Christ’s greatness is beyond expression.

The lowliness of the church on the other side is as deep. She is a created being, fashioned out of the earth, proceeding from the loins of corrupt Adam; not only finite, but in herself vile and base. The prophet Ezekiel#footnote[Ezek 16:1. #emph[etc.]] sets her forth in her true colors, as she is in herself. Compared to Christ, therefore, she is “nothing, less than nothing.”#footnote[Isa 40:17.] What equality, what proportion can there then be between Christ and her?

But if man and woman are compared together, we will find a near equality, and that both in the points of their humbling and in those of their exaltation. As to the former, they are both of the same mold, of the same corrupt nature, subject to the same infirmities, and at length brought to the same end. As to the latter, the best and greatest privileges are common to both of them: they are both made after the same image, redeemed by the same price, partakers of the same grace, and heirs together of the same inheritance.

#emph[Question.] What, then, is the preferment of the male sex? What is the excellence of a husband?

#emph[Answer.] Only outward and temporary. #emph[Outward,] in the things of this world only, for “in Christ Jesus” they are both “one.” #footnote[Gal 3:28.] #emph[Temporary,] for the time of this life only, for “in the resurrection, people will neither marry nor be given in marriage. Instead, they will be like the angels in heaven.”#footnote[Matt 22:30.] Then all subjection of wives to husbands ceases.

To conclude this point: since the inequality between Christ and the church, and the equality between man and wife, are as has been declared, and since Christ stoops to love his church, ought not a man to be moved by this to love his wife?

The other point, concerning the #emph[small benefit that Christ] #footnote[2\. The small benefit that Christ reaps by loving his church.] #emph[reaps by his church,] will enforce the point yet further. To illustrate it, we will note the great benefit that a man reaps by his wife.

The benefit that Christ reaps from the church is, in one word, #emph[nothing.] For Christ is in himself #emph[all-sufficient:] he neither needs anything nor can receive anything. “If you are righteous, what do you give Him, or what does He receive from your hand?”#footnote[Job 35:7.] Yet he abundantly bestows all manner of gifts, temporal and spiritual, earthly and heavenly. It was not, therefore, his own good that he had regard to in loving the church, but her good. For he, being #emph[God,]#footnote[1 Tim 3:16.] became #emph[man;] being #emph[Lord of heaven and earth,] he took on himself “the form of a servant”;#footnote[Phil 2:6, 7.] being #emph[rich,]#footnote[2 Cor 8:9.] he became #emph[poor;] holding “the keys of Death and of Hades,”#footnote[Rev 1:18.] and being #footnote[Acts 3:15.] the #emph[Lord of life,] he “humbled Himself and became obedient to death.”#footnote[Phil 2:8.] So, to show love to his church, he left much for her sake, but received nothing from her.

But the benefit that a man reaps from a wife is very great. For “it is not good for the man to be alone,”#footnote[Gen 2:18.] so much so that “he who finds a wife finds a good thing,”#footnote[Prov 18:22.] and that in all the points of goodness: a profitable thing, a comforting thing, a delightful thing. They do not know the benefit of the married state who #footnote[See Treatise 2, Part 1, §. 27.] prefer single life to it, especially if the married state is ordered by God’s word, and man and wife are careful to perform their own duty to each other.

To apply this point also, and bring it to its conclusion: #footnote[Christ’s example removes all the pretenses that men make for not loving their wives.] if Christ, who can receive nothing from the church, nevertheless loves her, ought not men much more to love their wives, since they receive much good from their wives in many ways, and cannot well do without them?

This example of Christ is all the more to be noted because it wholly wipes away all those false colors and vain pretenses that many allege as reasons to show that there is little reason they should love their wives. Some of their pretenses are these:

1\. Their wives are of a far lower rank than #footnote[1\. #emph[Objection.]] themselves; should they then perform duty to their inferiors? Those who marry their kitchen maids, or others far beneath their station, commonly allege this pretense.

#emph[Answer.] I might reply that marriage raises a wife to the rank of her husband, and that it was his own folly to marry one so lowly. But for the purpose and point in hand, let anyone tell me whether the supposed disparity between them and their wives is in any degree comparable to that which is between Christ and the church; yet Christ does not think it too much to do duties of love to his church.

2\. There is nothing in their wives worthy to be loved. #footnote[2\. #emph[Objection.]]

#emph[Answer.] This very thing, that #emph[such a one is your wife,] is matter enough to make her worthy of love. But what was there in the church to make her worthy of Christ’s love? If it is said that she is endowed with many excellent graces, which make her lovely in Christ’s sight, I answer that of herself she has none of those graces. Christ has bestowed them on her, and so made her lovely. And so you ought to endeavor, by using all the good means you can, to make your wife correspond to your love; but in any case, to love her.

3\. Their wives give just cause to be hated because of their #footnote[3\. #emph[Objection.]] irritability, stubbornness, insolence, and other such intolerable vices.

#emph[Answer.] No cause should seem just enough to move a husband to hate his wife, nor any vice seem intolerable to him: with good he ought to overcome evil. If notorious sins seemed intolerable to Christ, or if he thought any cause just enough to provoke hatred, many who are of his church would often draw his hatred on themselves. But Christ hates not one member of his church.

4\. There is no hope that I will ever receive any help from my #footnote[4\. #emph[Objection.]] wife, or any benefit from her.

#emph[Answer.] There is little love in those who can conceive no hope, for love “hopes all things.”#footnote[1 Cor 13:7.] Yet this is just the case with Christ. The church is so utterly unable to help or benefit him that he may justly say he cannot hope to receive anything from her. Christ loves the church for her own good, not for his; so ought husbands. Thus, if Christ’s example is well weighed and observed by husbands, it will afford matter enough to remove every doubt or scruple raised to turn their affections away from their wives. Fitly, therefore, has the apostle set it before husbands, both to direct them how to love their wives and also to move them to do so.

=== §. 76. #emph[Of a man’s love for himself, a motive to provoke him to love his wife.]

The pattern of a man’s self tends to the same purpose as Christ’s example. Great is the affection that a man bears to himself, to his own flesh, his own body. He never hates, but always loves himself. No sore, no disease, no pain, no stench that the flesh brings on a man can make him hate it; rather, all manner of infirmities make him pity it, tend it, and cherish it all the more. This is a work of nature: the most heathenish and barbarous people that ever were do it. Now since a wife is to a man as his body and his flesh (for “the two will be one flesh”), and since God has commanded men to “love their wives as their own bodies,” these conclusions will necessarily follow from this motive:

1\. He who does not love his wife is more led by the instinct of nature than by the express charge of the God of nature. Nature’s instinct moves him to love his body, but God’s express charge does not move him to love his wife.

2\. He who does not love his wife is worse than an unbeliever and a barbarian, indeed, worse than a beast. For all these love their own bodies and their own flesh; but a wife (by God’s ordinance) is as a man’s body and his flesh.

3\. “He who loves his wife loves himself.” The apostle himself #footnote[#emph[v.] 28.] lays down this conclusion in these very words. From this, by the rule of contraries, this will also follow: #emph[he who does not love his wife does not love himself.]

4\. He who does not love his wife cannot help but bring woe and injury on himself. For the damage and injury that falls on a wife through any neglect of duty on her husband’s part falls also on him, just as the injury that falls on the body through any negligence of the head falls also on the head.

If these are not motives enough to provoke a husband to love his wife, I do not know what can be enough.
